Z||000028||PearlBlueSoul||11-28-2000||03:21 AM||||HEY...Can I add some fuel to this fire?!<P>Alright, I'm the *******  who posted the thread when a Fiero person came to our board bashin'.  Bout him being an a$$hole n such.    8^/  Ignorance isn't always bliss (contrary to what the Matrix tells me    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).  <P>The site by the anonymous Fiero owner was posted up on our board some week and a half ago, and then after 2 of us e-mail him, here comes a Fiero owner bashin on us.  so, I go do a stupid thing and post a thread about it.  (silly seeing as I'm the moderator for our O/T forum).  I was particularly angry at that one individual, not the fiero community as a whole.  Hope that wasn't taken with false pretense.  I was just outraged by the ignorance displayed by the person who had put up that site.  I really hope he isn't among you guys.  That would really suck.  <P>Here's something I wrote to RealFiero, whoever that guy is....<P>Hey, Real, thanks for that post in the off topic forum man. <P>I understand what you're saying about the whole board war thing. It's strange how one post can kill the reputation of a whole society of car people (for lack of a better word). <P>the gentleman in question only posted on our Kill board and our first generation MR2 board, and they are in comparable light to the Fiero. These being made from 84-89, and if my memory serves they made the Fiero from 84-88, with 88 being the rarest years, as were the 89 S/C MR2s. I know the first MR2s had their share of faiures as well, such as driveline issues, but what car in it's first year hasn't? I think we take it a little too for granted that cars come without falt nowadays with the extensive amounts of testing they have to endure before they're ever allowed to enter the market. <P>I hope that we don't give off a bad representation of ourselves as a community, just as I'm sure you hope your reputation hasn't been ruined by a particular person from your side. Neither board I think enjoys a flame or bashing, so I think we see eye to eye on this one. Heck yah we could arrange some races! I would love to see that/take part in it. What all do you guys have out there in Fiero land in the 4 cylinder variety that could be good competition for my 87 N/A? <P>thanks again for the post, and I guess...I look forward to seeing some more posting from you in the future! <P>Oh, to end this on a more positive note...I've kidna had a crazy dream to do a 350 conversion, but BLOWN.  Not n/a, full supercharger with the triple butterflies, roller cams, rockers, lifters, you name it.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  heeh....I've just seen 350 fieros done before, but not one had a nice shiny blower on the top stickin through the decklid.  <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR><A HREF="http://"http://www.atlantamr2s.com"" TARGET=_blank>www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://"http://www.geocities.com/darionscard"" TARGET=_blank>Pics of my car, "Heaven and Hell"</A><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">   <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.<P>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 11-28-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||onfire||11-28-2000||04:04 AM||||Wow that took a half hour to read. Well it seemed to calm down at the end. It would be interesting to see mr2's and Fiero's at a show or a race (at the same time). <P>------------------<BR>"I seen it on the internet, and I believe everthing on the net."<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank>Check out my waist of banbwidth.</A>||||reg||1
Z||000030||RaeQwan||11-28-2000||04:21 AM||||Yet again, I learn new info bout my lovely lil plastic baby. I never knew it was the fuel lines is why Fiero's had engine fires, hell ya know, I guess GM should have put the fuel lines where everyone else does, Under the hood!  and I feel kinda cheated, my 88 didn't come supercharged like ya'll 84-87'ers did. I guess I'm lucky though, I got that lotus handling. ah well,I aint gonna muck with this one, will get me unhapy and make me unable to spell hehe..<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwancfa.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||PearlBlueSoul||11-28-2000||05:33 AM||||Actually, we weren't S/Ced untill 88-89 here in the states!  Japan had the S/C Mr2s in 1986!  Oh, and Lotus helped design our cars too.  there's some rumor that the MR2 was an abandoned Lotus project, but I don't think that one has ever been truly proven.<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||MR2 Fan||11-28-2000||08:49 AM||||Ok, I'm not going to start anything here...<BR>I was ChrisFL on the MR2 board. The reason why I wrote that loooonnngggg paragraph was because of a coversation with the owner of the MR2 bashing page, who at the time had really said some stupid things (as you probably know).<P>The thing is, the people who posted on the MR2 board kept saying that we should get our facts straight and that the fire recalls were only in 1984. Well, everybody knows that they were recalled for all years and not just the 4-cylinder configuration, as the Alldata.com information proved. Face it, the Fiero was known by the media to be a problematic car, and the people looked at it in comparison to the Corvair, another GM product. Fair? Maybe not, but that's what happened.<P>Yes, in 1985 the Fiero may have been slightly faster in a straight line than the MR2, and it is probably a toss-up in the road course or auto-x depending on the tires, driver, etc.<P>And with the V6 Fiero and MR2 S/C there were some handling differences from thier stock 4-cylinder brothers. <P>I just don't see what the advantages of a Fiero are, but I guess some of you do. Putting a Northstar V8 in a car and putting a  fake Ferrari bodykit on it may be appealing to you (and, yes there are Ferrari bodykits for the MR2 as well, MKI and MKII) but we MR2 owners are probably a different breed in some ways. And no, we don't eat rice or put it in our fuel tanks. The MKI has a very unique styling to it, and as said before, most likely a result of a Lotus design.<P>Us MKII owners are proud of Toyota for producing a truly supercar, with the help of racing legend Dan Gurney. Constantly compared to cars 5 times its cost, it has been in several presitigious racing events, and a V8 powered one was in LeMans in '96, possibly the most prestigious GT race in the world. <P>If you want flexibility to add a V8 easily to an inexpensive car, I guess the Fiero is for you, but if you want an excellent handling car, that is very dependable, retains a high resale value and has Toyota quality, then the MR2 is the best buy.<P>Heck, the MKII Turbo can be had for about $6,000 and you can tune that 4-cylinder to over 400hp with merely bolt-ons. Nearly 700hp can be put on that engine as Phoenix Power has proven.<P>You can have your Fiero, but I'll keep my MKII MR2, even if it isn't a Turbo.||||reg||14
Z||000033||Tigger||11-28-2000||09:19 AM||||Ohhh boy, that was a lot of fun reading this morning.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Wow what a mess.  Glad I bailed last night when I saw this was going to get out of hand.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||AkursedX||11-28-2000||10:22 AM||||You know, you don't go online for one evening and you miss all the fun!!  DAMN!  <P>Although I don't have anything against MR2's, I almost bought one, but I liked the looks of my '88 GT better, plus I have a better understanding of the engine.  <P>As far as which car is better, who is to really say?  I mean, if you have a good understanding of both of the cars, I think that you will find that they are too close to compare.  They both have their strengths and weaknesses, just like ANY other car.  <P>I know alot of people take alot of pride in their cars, and that is why they get so offended when someone else bashes them.  Especially us Fiero owners, who do we get respect from?  No one really.  The American car owners think we own a POS 'vette, and import owners think we own a wanna-be MR2.  And damn near everyone thinks we own a firestarting death trap.  But I'm ok with that.  Because #1, it makes our cars ALOT cheaper to buy, and #2, it always puts a smile on my face when you out-race someone who thinks you are driving nothing but a piece of crap.  <P>The Fiero has a bad name to the majority of America, plain and simple.  There are only 1600 some odd members on this board, but hundreds of thousands of Fiero's.  It's ineveitable that most Fieros will look like crap (I see maybe one good Fiero for about every 7 that I see, mine is a good one of couse  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )  On the other hand, I hardly see MR2's in my neck of the woods, but the ones that I see are usually better than most Fiero's I see.  <P>Ok, I think I'll get off my soapbox for now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>I'm a Fiero owner and I am damn proud of it, and I really don't give a crap about what everyone else says.<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000035||Spyro86SE||11-28-2000||10:31 AM||||i'm glad we are all able to get along <P>life is short live it to the fullest but have fun doin it <P><BR>love,peace and hair grease <P><p>[This message has been edited by Spyro86SE (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||Oreif||11-28-2000||10:52 AM||||I have driven a few MR2's in my time and of course Fiero's. Like it says above, each car has it's good points and weak points. I prefer the Fiero. If I am not mistaken, the Fiero is larger and heavier than the MR2. I always thought the Fiero was competition for the RX7. In fact IMSA did not have MR2's and Fiero's in the same class, They had Fiero's racing against RX7's. (Just for facts sake, Fiero's won 65% of the races from 1985 to 1988, the first IMSA GTU car to do so!) But I'm sure that there are many cars on both sides that are very fast, handle very well, and look fantastic.<P>I just don't think we should be joining each others forum's for the purpose of bashing.<BR> <BR>I do have a question for the MR2 folks,<BR>Have you ever driven a nice Fiero? Most people who I have talked to have either never driven one or drove a beat-up one.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000037||Jaygee79||11-28-2000||11:25 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MR2 Fan:<BR><B>Well, everybody knows that they were recalled for all years and not just the 4-cylinder configuration, as the Alldata.com information proved.<P>I just don't see what the advantages of a Fiero are.  And no, we don't eat rice or put it in our fuel tanks.<P>but if you want an excellent handling car, that is very dependable, retains a high resale value and has Toyota quality, then the MR2 is the best buy.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes there were recalls for every year.  Almost every car in existence has had some kind of recall.  If you keep up with them and take care of your car then it's not a problem.  I have a video tape here of a race between a Fiero and a MR2 and it shows that the Fiero is better not only in speed but also in handling, braking, looks, and comfort.<P>I won't lie, my car did catch on fire once, but it had nothing to do with any of the recalls.  My cousin decided to rewire my car for me and...well, you get the idea.<P>The only times that I have seen an MR2 (and I've seen quite a few around here) they are either broken down on the side of the road or ratty-looking with a For Sale sign on them.  I'm not putting down the car, but I have honestly never seen one running in good shape and I've never seen one in any of the races that I've been to.  Just my 2 cents.<P>And, by the way, the rice thing is just an expression.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Oreif||11-28-2000||12:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MR2 Fan:<BR><B>but if you want an excellent handling car, that is very dependable, retains a high resale value and has Toyota quality, then the MR2 is the best buy.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just for facts sake, Kelly Blue Book shows a fully loaded 1986 MR2 with a retail value of $2485.00 and a Fully loaded 1986 Fiero SE as $2670.00 and GT as 3245.00. So the resale is about the same. (given the original cost in 1986 and it's cost now). As for dependable, It doesn't matter what car you drive, If you take care of it and do the prescribed maintanence it will last a long, long time.<BR>I know of many cars that have over 200K on them that are dependable because the owner took care of the car. (american and japanese)<BR>Finally, per 3 different magazines from 1986, The Fiero was faster in the slalom than the MR2 and the Fiero had a higher skidpad rating. (Source: Car and Drive Aug 1986, Road and Track April 1986, Motortrend May 1986) I used the The same year cars as a comparison. (although even the redesigned MR2 in the early 90's still could not go thru the slalom faster than the Fiero)<BR>Also these are stock from factory cars. I know that either car (Fiero or MR2) have many aftermarket parts to improve handling and horsepower. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000039||stsmithgt||11-28-2000||12:10 PM||||That's the impression I got.  It sounds like the people that showed off their car were some what illiterate and neglected and/or abused their car too much.  I bet they didn't have the correct tire size on the car to help reduce the push problem.  The only noise I hear out of my friends is that grunt when they climb out of the car. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They usually have a smile on their faces if we were jetin' around.  Sometimes they look a little scared. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>FIERO = "MORE BANG FOR THE BUCK!"<P><BR>how much did those MR2's cost again?||||reg||1
Z||000040||StuGood||11-28-2000||01:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMaster88:<BR><B>...I dont see why Fiero owners and MR2 owners dont get along...  The Fiero just happens to be top on my list...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same here.  MR2's aren't for everybody; neither are Fieros.  So I'm not going to "sell" anyone on Fieros - everybody doesn't <B>have</B> to like them.<P>So, if someone out there dislikes Fieros, or  thinks MR2's are better cars than Fieros, I  offer this response:  Fine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, members, thanks for keeping your cool on the Forum.  That's what makes it such a great resource, both for the information and entertainment.  You are a great bunch of people  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <P>    <P>||||reg||6
Z||000041||btoth||11-28-2000||02:48 PM||||Woah.  I was worried when I saw all these posts, but relieved to see that I didn't start a war here.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But I am sorry that the MR2 bored got hit with a million flames from here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>BTW, my friend owns a '91 MR2.. very nice car!  Still have yet to race him though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000042||PearlBlueSoul||11-28-2000||03:03 PM||||OK, to clear up some more madness   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The MR2 in question that retains such a high resale value is the MKII MR2.  not the MKI (which is the same gen as the fieros).  a 91 Turbo MR2 can be had for usually no less than 6-7 grand in decent shape.  <P>I actually almost didn't become a MR2 fanatic, as my dad was looking at fieros beforehand.  Only problem was, every one we saw had problems.  I truly haven't seen a fiero in decent shape, let alone ridden in one where I live (GA).  So I can't honestly say that i know what I'm talking about when I don't.  A friend of mine almost bought a slightly damaged 88 formula v6.  At the time he had the lower output 2.8 in his firebird V6.  That thing had some torque!  his car would actually beat mine by a lil bit (but remember, 4 banger n/a, wrong class).  <P>I learned quite a bit from him too about what all the fieros had.  for a while there I never knew they came with 6 cylinder motors.  I know we're working on stuffing v6es in our MR2s from a late model Camry, and adding the TRD supercharger.  The group that's doing the swap is on the ATL MR2s page in my siggy.  <P>All is not ripe and well on our board either.  I can't say everyone's adjusting well, and I've already asked that those who are wishing to flame NOT come to your board and do the same.  that's just petty and stupid.  if you guys get flamed from some stupid MR2 person, I ask that you quickly remove him from your board.  I don't like to see that kinda shiznit.  We've both got alot we can learn from ourselves, and we've got alot to offer in the way of ingenuity and comroddery.  Do we all agree?  <P>I say one hearty scottish, AYE!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR><A HREF="http://"http://www.atlantamr2s.com"" TARGET=_blank>www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://"http://www.geocities.com/darionscard"" TARGET=_blank>Pics of my car, "Heaven and Hell"</A><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">   <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||Seanh||11-28-2000||03:38 PM||||I agree. I dont think anyone when over there to flame, I didnt. I just figured if they are going to poke crap at our cars, they should atleast get things straight about them. I mean rear radiators, come on? Im sure GM has done some pretty stupid things in the past but I dont believe rear radiators was ever one of them. ||||reg||1
Z||000044||Cliff Pennock||11-28-2000||03:55 PM||||I never understand why the <B>faster</B> car must be the <B>better</B> car. Hell, I don't even understand why a <B>better</B> car must the, well, <B>better</B> car (anyone still following me?).<P>I've owned cars that were "better" than both the Fiero and the MR2 in every aspect. For instance, I used to own a BMW 7-series which would do 150mph easily and do 0-60mph in around 6sec, and we all know BMW is a pretty reliable car. But I never felt any passion for that car, it was just a piece of machinery that brought me from point A to B.<BR>||||reg||1
