Z||000060||stimpy||11-29-2000||10:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MR2 Fan:<BR><B> Actually they were recalled for ALL years for engine fires...<P>Bulletins for 1984 Pontiac Fiero 4-151 2.5L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C23          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Fires <P>Bulletins for 1985 Pontiac Fiero 4-151 2.5L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C23          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Fires <P>Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C24          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Compartment Fires, Campaign 88-C-24 <P>Bulletins for 1986 Pontiac Fiero 4-151 2.5L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C23          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Fires <P>Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C24          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Compartment Fires, Campaign 88-C-24<P>Bulletins for 1987 Pontiac Fiero 4-151 2.5L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C23          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Fires<P>Bulletins for 1987 Pontiac Fiero V6-173 2.8L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C24          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Compartment Fires, Campaign 88-C-24 <P>Bulletins for 1988 Pontiac Fiero 4-151 2.5L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C23          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Fires <P>Bulletins for 1988 Pontiac Fiero V6-173 2.8L<P>                                          Safety Recalls<P>     TSB Number   Issue Date  TSB Title<BR>  1. 88C24          JUN 90       Potential For Engine Compartment Fires, Campaign 88-C-24 <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, fine. All Fieros are as combustable as gas-soaked dryer lint. Rolling molotov cocktails. Whatever. Fact is that I have 147,000 miles on my car and it has not even once caught on fire. <P>I challenge you, MR2 fan, to find me ONE car made in the last 20 years that has never had ANY recall campaigns.  I await your results.||||reg||1
Z||000061||MR2 Fan||11-29-2000||11:15 AM||||Well having a general recall (like the MKII's A/C not cooling properly) is a lot different than a Safety recall which can put everybody's lives in danger around that specific car. Engine fires are NOT a minor thing. if that fire were to spread to the gas tank...<P>Anyway, I've never driven a Fiero, but I was dating a girl a few years ago whose parents had 2 Fieros, and one night she locked the keys in her car (not a fiero) in another nearby city and her parents couldn't trust either Fiero on the highway, so she called me.<P>And most Fieros I see on the road have the "wink" going. I'm sorry if my experiences are not what most Fieros are like, but I've almost never heard any good things about them, until yesterday that is.<p>[This message has been edited by MR2 Fan (edited 11-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000062||Tigger||11-29-2000||01:08 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally Posted by Steve M - MR2 Forum:<BR>I don't think the Fiero burning problem is something that us MR2 owners can say because the MK1 MR2 has the exact same problem. The distributor will leak oil onto the exhaust manifold and the car will "go up in smoke"<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Guys, from what I'm reading over there, both of our cars can develop fire problems.  All cars have this risk, especially if they are not maintained.  Shoot, I seen a Chevy full size van on fire on the side of the interstate last week...  does this mean they all burn up? prob not.<P>I've seen just about ever car with flip up headlights doing the "wink" thing.  So?<P>Let's give it a rest.  This whole thing was started by some lame Fiero owner with an anti-MR2 site than none of us even knew about (at least I didn't) till now.<P>It's great to see guys like PearlBlue over here and fellow members and myself giving all sorts of compliments to each others cars.  I respect these kinds of people because they understand the differences in cars, peoples tastes, and are not stuck on just one car, the one they own. <P>I think most of us have come to an understanding of what caused all of this and it already appears as if something good did come from all this.  It looks like we've already had a couple members from both boards meeting to talk about and learn more about each others cars.  Try that between a couple of Mustang and Camaro owners, sheesh!<P>Ok, I've said enough for now     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000063||VelociFero||12-01-2000||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>OREIF wrote:  I do have a question for the MR2 folks,<BR>Have you ever driven a nice Fiero? Most people who I have talked to have either never driven one or drove a beat-up one.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My Fiero is in better shape than my MR2 (which is in GREAT condition).  On a side-by-side comparison, I choose my MkII, but that is primarily because of the Auto Transmission on the Fiero.  Anybody near DeKalb, IL that has never driven either a nice 86 GT Fiero or a MkII MR2 is welcome to shoot me an email.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>1991 2.2L MR2<BR>1986 GT Fiero <BR>00 Mustang (Black)<BR>00 Mustang (Silver)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2pages.com/members/velocifero/SmallMR2one.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000064||rockcrawl||12-01-2000||08:26 PM|||| I hate to get involved in this stupid dispute but....<BR> I've never owned or driven an MR2 so I cannot say which is a better car. I am a full time mechanic at a Toyota dealer, and I can say that the quality of a Toyota is very good. I would venture to say that a new Toyota is better built than say a new Chevrolet of the same price range. But still I have never owned a Toyota and probably never will (well, I'd love to have an old FJ40 landcruiser!). They just do not appeal to me for whatever reason. I have owned at least 10 Fieros. I meet people every day that are Fiero-bashers but they have never driven or owned a Fiero. Thats like saying you don't like the taste of brocholi without ever tasing it. Every Fiero owner I meet loves their car. I think a lot of MR2 fans are comparing second and third generation to fieros, which just isn't right. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000065||VelociFero||12-01-2000||11:01 PM||||I only compare the two cars as an owner.<BR>They stack up pretty well against one another considering the 5 year age split and the price difference.  Plus, I am comparing my pretty-well-modified Deuce against my showroom-stock Fiero.  <BR>Again, the real clincher is, for me, the Automatic tranny... I have to pick ratios for myself <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>1991 2.2L MR2<BR>1986 GT Fiero <BR>00 Mustang (Black)<BR>00 Mustang (Silver)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2pages.com/members/velocifero/SmallMR2one.jpg"> ||||reg||1
